---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

/* THE PAGE FOR SOMEONE COMPARING AGAINST A HOSTED FREE TIER.
 *
 * Written against docs/notes/competitor-facts-2026-08-15.md, which is the only
 * sanctioned source for anything on this page that describes restream.io. Every
 * figure below traces to a line in it. Nothing about their product is written
 * from memory, from their marketing, or from what a competitor's free tier
 * "usually" includes.
 *
 * WHY THE DATE IS RENDERED RATHER THAN KEPT IN A COMMENT. A price on a
 * competitor's plan is not a stale docs line when it goes out of date — it is a
 * false statement about another company's prices, which is a different class of
 * problem. The stamp is in the markup, twice, around every figure that can move:
 * once on the section, once in the table caption. If someone rechecks, they
 * change one constant and both move together; if nobody rechecks, a reader can
 * see how old the numbers are and go and look.
 *
 * NO FAQPage JSON-LD, though the headings below are literally questions. The
 * home page's "Is this for you?" block was considered for it and rejected for
 * being bullet lists rather than question-and-answer pairs; this block would
 * pass that test and still fails a different one. Marking up a competitor's
 * pricing as structured data invites a search engine to reproduce those figures
 * in a result card, detached from the "checked" stamp that is the only thing
 * making them honest. The claims stay on the page, next to their date. */
const CHECKED = "2026-08-15";

/* restream.io plans, from facts file §2.D. Read as: what the money buys, in the
 * terms this page is about. Deliberately NOT a full feature list — this page is
 * not a review of their product and cannot be, on one sourced document. */
const plans = [
  {
    name: "Free",
    price: "$0",
    channels: "2 simultaneous channels",
    detail:
      "Personal profiles and platforms only. Excludes custom RTMP destinations, and excludes Facebook Pages and Groups. Restream watermark on Studio output, capped at 720p.",
  },
  {
    name: "Standard",
    price: "$19/mo",
    channels: "3 channels",
    detail:
      "$16/mo billed annually, at $190/yr. No watermark, and custom RTMP destinations are enabled.",
  },
  {
    name: "Professional",
    price: "$49/mo",
    channels: "5 channels",
    detail:
      "$39/mo billed annually, at $470/yr. 1080p Studio, and dual-format output — horizontal and vertical at once.",
  },
];

/* The self-hosting bill, itemised. This section is the reason the page exists in
 * the shape it does: a reader arriving on "free restream service" has already
 * been sold something free once today, and a page that lists only what
 * self-hosting removes reads as the same pitch with a different logo. Every
 * entry below is a cost we have actually met, sourced from the install and
 * quickstart docs rather than imagined. */
const costs = [
  {
    title: "A box, and its upstream",
    body: "One upload leaves your studio; the fan-out leaves the server. So the server needs roughly your bitrate multiplied by the number of destinations, in sustained upstream. CPU is rarely the wall — video is stream-copied, and a destination measures at about 4% of one core — but bandwidth is, and a domestic connection is usually the wrong place to put it.",
  },
  {
    title: "A domain and a certificate, if it leaves the LAN",
    body: "The installer fits a self-signed certificate unless told otherwise, which browsers will complain about. Let’s Encrypt needs a hostname that resolves and an email address, and the installer refuses to proceed without both. Behind a proxy that already terminates TLS, you turn it off here instead.",
  },
  {
    title: "An FFmpeg with the right things in it",
    body: "FFmpeg 6.0 or newer, and it needs libsrt for the multitrack SRT ingest that makes per-destination audio possible at all. Homebrew’s macOS build ships without SRT. Ubuntu 24.04’s stock 6.1.1 carries SRT but predates multitrack FLV. The container bundles a known-good build and sidesteps the question entirely.",
  },
  {
    title: "Your own time, and your own pager",
    body: "There is no status page to refresh and no support queue to sit in. polyemesis ships Prometheus metrics and alert rules with webhook delivery so that a stream which stops can page somebody — but the somebody is you, and at 3am nobody else is on call. That is the single honest difference between this and a subscription, and it is not a small one.",
  },
  {
    title: "Linux, for anything you care about",
    body: "The broadcast path is tested on Windows and macOS in CI, and that suite has caught Windows-only bugs before they shipped. What is not proven on Windows is long-running service operation: the service wrapper is unexercised and recording can truncate on service stop. Build and evaluate anywhere; operate on Linux.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="A free restream service you run yourself"
  description="Restream’s free tier allows two channels and watermarks Studio. polyemesis is MIT, self-hosted and caps nothing — and here is what that trade costs."
  path="/free-restream-service"
>
  <section class="wrap py-16 sm:py-20">
    <p class="eyebrow">Free restream service</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-4xl text-balance text-4xl font-bold leading-tight sm:text-5xl">
      A free restream service, if you are willing to run the server.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
      Restream does have a free tier, and it allows two simultaneous channels.
      If that is the answer you came for, it is at the top of the page on
      purpose. The rest of this is the other answer: polyemesis is a restreaming
      server that is free in the plain sense — MIT licensed, no account, no
      hosted tier, nothing phoning home — because it runs on a machine you
      already pay for. That machine is the price, and this page is mostly about
      what it costs.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a href="/download" class="btn btn-primary">Install it</a>
      <a href="#the-trade" class="btn btn-ghost">What it costs you</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------ what is a restream --- */}
  <section id="what-it-is" class="border-t border-line py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="wrap max-w-3xl">
      <p class="eyebrow">The word</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 text-balance text-2xl font-medium sm:text-3xl">
        What a restream service actually is
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
        You send one stream out of your encoder. Something downstream copies it
        and delivers a copy to every platform you named — Twitch, YouTube, Kick,
        Facebook, Rumble — so your uplink carries one broadcast instead of five.
        That is the whole job, and every product in this category does it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
        The word also does double duty as a brand. Restream, at restream.io, is
        a proprietary hosted service that does this in their cloud on a monthly
        subscription. If you searched for a free restream service you are
        probably weighing one against the other, so both are on this page: what
        their free tier gives you, and what running the fan-out yourself gives
        you instead.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Where products in this category diverge is not the copying. It is what
        happens to the audio on the way, and whether the fan-out runs on
        somebody else’s hardware or on yours.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------- is restream free? --- */}
  <section id="is-restream-free" class="border-t border-line bg-surface/30 py-16 sm:py-20" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap max-w-4xl">
      <p class="eyebrow">The direct answer</p>
      <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-3">
        <h2 class="text-balance text-2xl font-medium sm:text-3xl">Is Restream free?</h2>
        <span class="chip">checked {CHECKED}</span>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Partly. There is a free plan and it allows two simultaneous channels, to
        personal profiles and platforms. What it does not include is the part
        most people find out later: custom RTMP destinations are excluded, so a
        platform Restream has no built-in integration for is not reachable on
        it, and Facebook Pages and Groups are excluded as well. Anything you
        produce in their browser Studio carries a Restream watermark and is
        capped at 720p.
      </p>

      {/* THE DATE SITS OUTSIDE THE SCROLL BOX. A <caption> inherits the table's
          min-width, so on a phone the sentence qualifying every price in the
          table would itself be behind a horizontal scroll — the one line here
          that must not be missable. The caption stays, short, for the screen
          reader; the warning is prose the page can lay out. */}
      <p class="mt-8 text-sm leading-relaxed text-subtle">
        Prices move. These were read off restream.io’s published pricing on
        {" "}{CHECKED}; if that stamp is old by the time you are reading it, go
        and look before you rely on any of it.
      </p>
      <div class="scroll-hint mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-token border border-line">
        <table class="cmp w-full min-w-[46rem] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
          <caption class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-[11px] text-subtle">
            restream.io plans, checked {CHECKED}
          </caption>
          <thead class="bg-surface">
            <tr class="border-b border-line">
              <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Plan</th>
              <th scope="col" class="w-28 px-4 py-3 font-medium">Price</th>
              <th scope="col" class="w-44 px-4 py-3 font-medium">Channels</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">What that includes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              plans.map((p) => (
                <tr class="border-b border-line/60 last:border-0">
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-fg">{p.name}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-muted">{p.price}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted">{p.channels}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">{p.detail}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-subtle">
        Every figure above is recorded with its source in this repository rather
        than paraphrased from a competitor’s marketing, and it is dated because a
        stale price is not a stale docs line — it is a false statement about
        another company’s prices. If one of these has moved, that is a bug in
        this page and we would like to be told.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------ what free means here - */}
  <section id="free-here" class="border-t border-line py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="wrap max-w-3xl">
      <p class="eyebrow">The other kind of free</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 text-balance text-2xl font-medium sm:text-3xl">
        No plan, because there is nothing to bill for.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
        polyemesis is MIT licensed and ships as one static binary or one
        container. There is no account to create, no free tier to graduate out
        of, and no telemetry — it can publish its own state over MQTT, but that
        is off by default and goes to a broker you name. The only runtime
        dependency is FFmpeg, and the container bundles it.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock
        label="docker"
        code={`docker run -d \\
  --name polyemesis \\
  -p 8080:8080 \\
  -p 6000:6000/udp \\
  -v polyemesis-data:/data \\
  rainmanjam/polyemesis`}
      />
      <dl class="mt-8 divide-y divide-line border-y border-line">
        {
          [
            [
              "Destinations",
              "No configured cap. There is no limit in the software, but upload bandwidth and CPU are real: a destination measures at roughly 4% of one core, so a four-core box runs out somewhere near 96. “No cap” is not the same word as “unlimited”, and the difference is the part that bites.",
            ],
            [
              "Custom RTMP",
              "Every destination is a custom RTMP destination. There is no list of blessed platforms, because there is no integration tier to sell — a URL and a key is all a destination has ever been.",
            ],
            [
              "Watermark",
              "None, anywhere. There is nothing on the page to advertise, and no plan above this one to advertise it to.",
            ],
            [
              "Your video",
              "Stream-copied by default, so the picture that reaches each platform is the one your encoder made. Optional shared renditions re-encode it, once, for the platforms that refuse your source.",
            ],
          ].map(([t, d]) => (
            <div class="py-4">
              <dt class="text-[15px] font-medium text-fg">{t}</dt>
              <dd class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{d}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------ the bill - */}
  <section id="the-trade" class="border-t border-line bg-surface/30 py-16 sm:py-20" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The trade</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-balance text-2xl font-medium sm:text-3xl">
        The subscription does not disappear. It changes currency.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Self-hosting removes the per-channel tier, the watermark and the monthly
        invoice. It replaces them with five things, and you should price all
        five before you install anything. If any one of them reads as a dealbreaker,
        a hosted service is the correct answer and this page has done its job.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
        {
          costs.map((c) => (
            <div class="card p-6">
              <h3 class="text-[15px] font-medium text-fg">{c.title}</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{c.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* --------------------------------------------------------- the reason -- */}
  <section id="the-audio" class="border-t border-line py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="wrap max-w-3xl">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why anyone accepts that trade</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 text-balance text-2xl font-medium sm:text-3xl">
        One thing no plan on the list above sells.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
        restream.io duplicates the single ingested stereo audio track
        identically across every enabled endpoint. Read the plans above again
        for what the money buys: channels, a resolution, the removal of a
        watermark. None of them is an audio plan, so on any of them the mix that
        goes to Twitch is the mix that goes to YouTube.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
        polyemesis takes the split audio tracks your encoder already sends —
        music on one, game on another, microphone on a third — and builds a
        different mix per destination from them, on the server, from one upload.
        The platform that mutes music gets the clean feed while the one that does
        not gets the full one, and neither costs you a second video encode.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
        That is the whole reason to run a server rather than rent one. If you do
        not need it, the honest recommendation is to weigh the five costs above
        against a subscription and pick whichever you would rather pay.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        <a href="/features" class="inline-block py-1 text-sm text-primary underline-offset-4 hover:underline">
          How the routing works →
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap border-t border-line py-20 text-center">
    <h2 class="text-balance text-3xl font-medium">Five minutes to a running server.</h2>
    <p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-xl text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
      Docker, an installer script, or a single static binary. Then point your
      encoder at it.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
      <a href="/download" class="btn btn-primary">Install</a>
      <a href="/how-to-multistream-from-obs" class="btn btn-ghost">Set up OBS</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
